2026-09-23T17:06:46.2667263Z     
2026-09-23T17:06:46.2667441Z     The ``pytest`` framework makes it easy to write small tests, yet
2026-09-23T17:06:46.2667658Z     scales to support complex functional testing for applications and libraries.
2026-09-23T17:06:46.2667738Z     
2026-09-23T17:06:46.2667842Z     An example of a simple test:
2026-09-23T17:06:46.2667918Z     
2026-09-23T17:06:46.2668019Z     .. code-block:: python
2026-09-23T17:06:46.2668098Z     
2026-09-23T17:06:46.2668197Z         # content of test_sample.py
2026-09-23T17:06:46.2668288Z         def inc(x):
2026-09-23T17:06:46.2668382Z             return x + 1
2026-09-23T17:06:46.2668462Z     
2026-09-23T17:06:46.2668538Z     
2026-09-23T17:06:46.2668631Z         def test_answer():
2026-09-23T17:06:46.2668730Z             assert inc(3) == 5
2026-09-23T17:06:46.2668805Z     
2026-09-23T17:06:46.2668886Z     
2026-09-23T17:06:46.2668976Z     To execute it::
2026-09-23T17:06:46.2669050Z     
2026-09-23T17:06:46.2669136Z         $ pytest
2026-09-23T17:06:46.2669307Z         ============================= test session starts =============================
2026-09-23T17:06:46.2669401Z         collected 1 items
2026-09-23T17:06:46.2669476Z     
2026-09-23T17:06:46.2669572Z         test_sample.py F
2026-09-23T17:06:46.2669653Z     
2026-09-23T17:06:46.2669793Z         ================================== FAILURES ===================================
2026-09-23T17:06:46.2669965Z         _________________________________ test_answer _________________________________
2026-09-23T17:06:46.2670044Z     
2026-09-23T17:06:46.2670135Z             def test_answer():
2026-09-23T17:06:46.2670232Z         >       assert inc(3) == 5
2026-09-23T17:06:46.2670327Z         E       assert 4 == 5
2026-09-23T17:06:46.2670422Z         E        +  where 4 = inc(3)
2026-09-23T17:06:46.2670495Z     
2026-09-23T17:06:46.2670607Z         test_sample.py:5: AssertionError
2026-09-23T17:06:46.2670760Z         ========================== 1 failed in 0.04 seconds ===========================
2026-09-23T17:06:46.2670835Z     
2026-09-23T17:06:46.2671027Z     
2026-09-23T17:06:46.2671779Z     Thanks to ``pytest``'s detailed assertion introspection, you can simply use plain ``assert`` statements. See `getting-started <https://docs.pytest.org/en/stable/getting-started.html#our-first-test-run>`_ for more examples.
2026-09-23T17:06:46.2671859Z     
2026-09-23T17:06:46.2671935Z     
2026-09-23T17:06:46.2672020Z     Features
2026-09-23T17:06:46.2672105Z     --------
2026-09-23T17:06:46.2672179Z     
2026-09-23T17:06:46.2672649Z     - Detailed info on failing `assert statements <https://docs.pytest.org/en/stable/how-to/assert.html>`_ (no need to remember ``self.assert*`` names)
2026-09-23T17:06:46.2672802Z     
2026-09-23T17:06:46.2672899Z     - `Auto-discovery
2026-09-23T17:06:46.2673183Z       <https://docs.pytest.org/en/stable/explanation/goodpractices.html#python-test-discovery>`_
2026-09-23T17:06:46.2673287Z       of test modules and functions
2026-09-23T17:06:46.2673368Z     
2026-09-23T17:06:46.2673754Z     - `Modular fixtures <https://docs.pytest.org/en/stable/explanation/fixtures.html>`_ for
2026-09-23T17:06:46.2673934Z       managing small or parametrized long-lived test resources
2026-09-23T17:06:46.2674017Z     
2026-09-23T17:06:46.2674280Z     - Can run `unittest <https://docs.pytest.org/en/stable/how-to/unittest.html>`_ (or trial)
2026-09-23T17:06:46.2674376Z       test suites out of the box
2026-09-23T17:06:46.2674456Z     
2026-09-23T17:06:46.2674555Z     - Python 3.10+ or PyPy3
2026-09-23T17:06:46.2674629Z     
2026-09-23T17:06:46.2675112Z     - Rich plugin architecture, with over 1300+ `external plugins <https://docs.pytest.org/en/latest/reference/plugin_list.html>`_ and thriving community
2026-09-23T17:06:46.2675192Z     
